cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this benchmark, please cite the paper and this repository."
title: "Error Propagation in Cascaded Speech Translation under Acoustic Channel Degradations: A Black-Box Case Study of Commercial Speech Intelligence APIs"
type: dataset
authors:
  - name: CascadeBench Authors
    website: "https://github.com/43aquarius"
keywords:
  - speech recognition
  - speech translation
  - robustness benchmark
  - acoustic channel degradation
  - black-box API evaluation
  - error propagation
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/43aquarius/CascadeBench-Paper-AppliedAcoustics"
abstract: >-
  CascadeBench is a fully seeded, black-box case study measuring how fourteen
  acoustic channel degradations from seven families propagate through the
  recognition and translation services of one commercial provider: five
  recognition systems in three product classes, three cascaded and two
  end-to-end translation paths, 480 bilingual sentences, and 43,046 logged
  API responses on complimentary quotas. A 150-utterance real-speech
  validation with a six-level real-babble ladder, measured room impulse
  responses, an open-weights recogniser (Whisper small), and a
  family-disjoint second-vendor reference and backend (GLM) replicate the
  knee-and-collapse structure, bound the audio-LLM advantage to the
  near-knee regime, and validate the recognition-to-translation
  pass-through relation beyond the provider's own stack.
version: 1.5.1
date-released: "2026-10-20"
